{
 "schema": "bie.brain.simple-hindi/1",
 "version": "2026-10-24.1",
 "status": "Everyday words (Task 045b seed, extended after the tissue run on 23 October 2026: 336 everyday words had been flagged as difficult). Navin adds words: put them in 'words' and bump the version.",
 "source": "Navin, 23 October 2026: narration is mixed script; a Hindi word NOT in this list of simple everyday words gets its English word in brackets right after it, for example 'सतह (surface)'; subtitles show the bracket, the voice says 'सतह, यानी surface'. Navin, 23 October 2026: brackets only on genuinely difficult words (formal, literary or technical), never inside an idiom or a fixed phrase (not 'आम तौर (usually) पर', not 'नहीं के बराबर (equal to)').",
 "how_it_is_used": [
  "bie/brain/hinglish.py compares every Devanagari word of a script beat with 'words', also after removing one common ending (ों, ें, ता, ते, कर, ेंगे, िए, ...) and trying the verb (+ना) or the noun (+ा, +ी, +े) form. A word not found must have its English word in brackets right after it.",
  "Words inside a 'fixed_phrases' entry are never difficult; a bracket inside one, or after an everyday word, is removed by BIE's code when the lesson is written (the maker is not sent back for it).",
  "Nukta and chandrabindu are ignored when comparing (ज़रा = जरा, आँख = आंख).",
  "English words in Roman script are never checked against this list and never have to be written in Devanagari.",
  "'technical' (Task 045d, 24 October 2026, from job A's first live page): the textbook Hindi translations of the book's own English terms. In an unpack (the plain meaning, image, example) and in a spoken beat the learner hears the book's term in Roman (tissue, cell, epithelium), never ऊतक, कोशिका, उपकला; a bracket does not rescue it. BIE's code sends the item or beat back."
 ],
